# SYNOPSIS

```usage
crash-test run [files]
crash-test verify
```

# DESCRIPTION

`crash-test` checks that a Taisce volume on a real disk keeps what it said was durable when the power is cut, as the crash harness checks every write prefix on the dev machine.
It works in `/data/crash-test`, fs(4)'s volume; the board's desktop image carries it, to run from a cpu(8) session, whose output stays on the dev machine when the board loses power.

`run` removes the last run's files, then writes <files> files (2000 if not given), `f0`, `f1` and on, each of 4 to 256 KiB whose bytes come from its number, in writes of at most 32 KiB.
After every eight it commits a transaction group (the directory's `sync`) and, once that returns, prints `crash-test: durable <n>`: the first <n> files are on the disk.
Then the power is cut, at any moment.

`verify`, after the next boot, reads every file back from `f0` until 32 numbers in a row are missing, and judges each: whole (its bytes, all of them), partial (a correct start; its later writes were in groups that didn't commit, as fs commits on its own a second after a change), empty, or wrong (a byte that isn't its own, a file longer than its own, or a number missing below one found).
It prints `crash-test: verify: <n> whole in a row from f0, <n> partial or empty, <n> wrong, <n> whole after a gap: passed` or `FAILED`, and exits 0 if it passed.
It passes when nothing is wrong and no whole file comes after one that isn't: groups commit in order.
A cut passes when it does and the whole files in a row number at least the last `durable` printed before the cut; the board's fs mounts with `--check`, so its line on the console says whether the volume's invariants held too.

# EXAMPLES

On the Orange Pi 6 Plus, from the dev machine, a cycle: boot the desktop image, then

```text
$ vx cpu -c 'crash-test verify' 192.168.0.19
$ vx cpu -c 'crash-test run' 192.168.0.19 | tee run.out
```

cut the power while it runs, and after the next boot compare `verify`'s count with the last `durable` in `run.out`.

